---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import OursStageExplorer from '../../../components/OursStageExplorer.astro';
import StageSpectrum from '../../../components/StageSpectrum.astro';
import ExperimentStatus from '../../../components/ExperimentStatus.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../../utils/paths';
const image = (name: string) => withBase('ours/ffhq64-a1-d256/' + name);
---
<Base title="Next-frequency generation | Research projects" description="A1 next-frequency generation: matched CIFAR-10 verdict, saved FFHQ-64 evaluation, cumulative stage samples, and limits.">
  <article>
    <header class="project-hero shell">
      <div class="hero-meta"><span>Our method / A1 next-frequency</span><span>CIFAR-10 D192 · FFHQ-64 D256</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-intro"><h1>Next-frequency generation.</h1><p class="hero-subtitle">An image, built one frequency group at a time.</p></div>
        <div class="hero-result" aria-label="Matched CIFAR-10 result">
          <p class="result-context">CIFAR-10 <span>·</span> D192 <span>·</span> A1 versus A0 at 100k</p>
          <div class="hero-metric"><strong>≈5%</strong><span>relative<br />FID cost</span></div>
          <p class="hero-criterion"><span class="criterion-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Pre-registered STRONG criterion met</p>
          <p class="hero-result-source">This is the CIFAR-10 result. The FFHQ-64 samples below are a separate D256 experiment with a different verdict.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-deck"><p class="hero-contribution">A1 predicts successive frequency groups instead of all DCT channels jointly. Its cumulative reconstruction can be inspected after each group.</p><a class="text-link" href="#samples">Follow the four stages <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-figure"><a href={image('a1-grid-146k.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open A1 FFHQ-64 generated sample grid"><img src={image('a1-grid-146k.png')} alt="A1 D256 generated FFHQ-64 faces from the 146k checkpoint" fetchpriority="high" /></a><figcaption><span>Saved A1 sample grid</span><span>FFHQ-64 · D256 · 146k steps · separate from the CIFAR-10 headline result</span></figcaption></figure>
    </header>

    <div class="shell story-grid">
      <aside class="story-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span class="nav-label">On this page</span><a href="#question">Method and question</a><a href="#cifar">CIFAR-10 result</a><a href="#samples">Stage samples</a><a href="#ffhq">FFHQ-64 result</a><a href="#limits">Limits and next steps</a></aside>
      <div class="story-content">
        <section id="question" class="story-section question-section">
          <p class="section-overline">The research question</p><h2>Can ordering frequencies preserve image quality?</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">The joint A0 control predicts all DCT channels together. A1 predicts four successive groups, G0–G3, exposing a cumulative image after each stage.</p>
          <div class="question-columns"><div><h3>What changes</h3><p>The FFHQ-64 A1 D256 model has four groups of 6, 12, 18 and 18 channels. Its sampler uses 100 network calls per group, 400 in total. D256 is the model width; the generated image is 64 × 64 pixels.</p></div><div><h3>What must be tested</h3><p>Inspect the intermediate reconstructions and measure fidelity at a declared comparison point. The CIFAR-10 and FFHQ-64 studies use different architectures and comparison protocols, so their verdicts stay separate.</p></div></div>
        </section>

        <section id="cifar" class="story-section result-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">CIFAR-10 / D192 / matched 100k</p><span class="evidence-tag">Reported result</span></div>
          <h2>STRONG at approximately 5% FID cost.</h2>
          <div class="result-composition"><div class="result-number"><strong>≈5%</strong><span>A1 FID cost relative to A0</span></div><div class="result-reading"><p>The next-frequency variant met the pre-registered <strong>STRONG</strong> criterion at matched 100k training steps.</p><span>This is a relative result. Exact A0 and A1 FID values are not available on this site.</span></div></div>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="CIFAR-10 A0 and A1 comparison" tabindex="0"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Arm</th><th scope="col">Matched setting</th><th scope="col">Reported outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A0 · joint control</th><td data-label="Matched setting">100k</td><td data-label="Reported outcome">FID reference</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A1 · next-frequency</th><td data-label="Matched setting">100k</td><td data-label="Reported outcome">≈5% relative FID cost; STRONG met</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="result-footnote">Qualitative caveat: a persistent single-token G2 checkerboard artifact was reported in seeds 5 and 7. Its figure is unavailable here, so the failure is stated without a substitute image.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="samples" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">FFHQ-64 / A1 D256</p><span class="evidence-tag">Saved 146k stage samples</span></div>
          <h2>One face through four cumulative stages.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">Choose a fixed sample and move from G0 to G0–G3. The source sheet contains eight generated faces across four cumulative reconstructions.</p>
          <OursStageExplorer />
          <StageSpectrum />
          <a class="text-link" href={withBase('projects/generation-step-by-step/#instruments')}>Compare this trajectory with DCTdiff and FAR in the streaming report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </section>

        <section id="ffhq" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">FFHQ-64 / matched H100-hours</p><span class="evidence-tag">Saved FID-50K evaluation</span></div>
          <h2>A1 beats the joint DCT control, but trails the pixel baseline.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">Each arm used about 3.34 H100 hours. Checkpoint steps differ because throughput differs. FID-50K uses the q90 real reference pool.</p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="FFHQ-64 matched-compute FID results" tabindex="0"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Arm</th><th scope="col">Checkpoint</th><th scope="col">FID-50K ↓</th><th scope="col">Sampler calls</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">R0 · pixel baseline</th><td data-label="Checkpoint">250k</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">12.66</td><td data-label="Sampler calls">100</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A0 · joint DCT</th><td data-label="Checkpoint">174k</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">18.49</td><td data-label="Sampler calls">100</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A1 · next-frequency D256</th><td data-label="Checkpoint">146k</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">15.08</td><td data-label="Sampler calls">400</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="result-footnote">A1 / R0 FID ratio is 1.19, above this study’s pre-registered 1.10 competitiveness threshold. The CIFAR-10 STRONG finding is a separate result. The A1 run is marked crashed after saving the 146k evaluation; no later outcome is asserted. <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/wqa3lin4">A1 run ↗</a> · <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/229cfxlk">A0 run ↗</a> · <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/3aanusfn">R0 run ↗</a></p>
          <div class="ours-pair"><figure><img src={image('a0-grid-100k.png')} alt="A0 joint DCT generated FFHQ-64 samples at 100k steps" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A0 · joint DCT · 100k steps</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={image('a1-grid-100k.png')} alt="A1 next-frequency generated FFHQ-64 samples at 100k steps" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A1 · next-frequency · 100k steps</figcaption></figure></div>
          <p class="ours-pair-note">These grids compare equal optimizer steps and different generated draws; they are not paired by seed. At 100k, monitoring FID-10K was 25.17 for A0 and 20.09 for A1. That smaller-sample diagnostic is distinct from the matched-compute FID-50K table.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="limits" class="story-section status-section">
          <p class="section-overline">Current verdict and limits</p><h2>Two studies, two verdicts.</h2>
          <div class="status-rows"><div><ExperimentStatus status="reported" /><p>CIFAR-10 D192 at 100k met STRONG with approximately 5% relative FID cost; exact FIDs and the seeds 5 and 7 G2 artifact figure are still unavailable here.</p></div><div><ExperimentStatus status="completed" /><p>The FFHQ-64 D256 146k evaluation and stage sheet are saved. A1 FID-50K is 15.08 and misses the A1/R0 ≤1.10 threshold.</p></div><div><ExperimentStatus status="proposed" /><p>Inspect the CIFAR-10 G2 artifact directly and report exact matched FIDs; evaluate any later FFHQ-64 checkpoint only if a saved evaluation becomes available.</p></div></div>
          <div class="end-note"><span>Source boundary</span><p>FFHQ-64 images and metrics are from the linked W&B runs; image hashes and full precision values are recorded in <a href={image('source.json')}>the source manifest</a>. The CIFAR-10 summary and G2 caveat come from the separate matched experiment.</p></div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
